And you want to see
what happened exactly

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.630
before this error message, what
led up to this error message.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.660
And this is the kind of view
where you would do that,

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:46.865
exploring the data.

00:09:46.865 --> 00:09:48.240
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yep, absolutely.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:48.870
Makes a lot of sense.

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:50.070
CHRISTOPHER WURM: Yeah.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.220
Now exploring the raw data
is one way to look at it.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:55.620
And then the other way that
you can look at your data

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:58.740
is to aggregate, to
look at the big picture.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.159
And so what we can do is we
can build visualizations.

00:10:01.159 --> 00:10:02.700
And then out of
these visualizations,

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:04.057
we can build dashboards.

00:10:04.057 --> 00:10:05.890
And let's look at what
that looks like here.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:11.190
So simple visualization
is always the pie chart.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:13.890
Here we can see this pie chart
is showing the different HTTP

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.200
response codes that we
see in our web logs.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:18.990
So the majority of
response codes are 200.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.010
So everything's fine.

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:21.220
That's good.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:27.600
But then we also have a sizable,
30%, of web logs that are 404.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.930
So people or applications
trying to access

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.990
part of a website that we--

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.234
and this request failed.

00:10:35.234 --> 00:10:37.650
And so we might want to explore
this a little bit further.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:38.700
What kind of
requests were these?

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:39.825
Where are they coming from?

00:10:39.825 --> 00:10:42.490
What IP addresses, what
URLs, what resources

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:45.030
did these people try to access?

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:47.864
And so for that, you
probably want a dashboard.

00:10:47.864 --> 00:10:49.280
And here we have
such a dashboard.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.690
It's showing a lot
of different things,

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:54.530
things like where our
requests coming from.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.160
We have a map up here.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:58.020
But also how many
requests do we have?

00:10:58.020 --> 00:10:59.445
14 million.

00:10:59.445 --> 00:11:01.570
And then various other
charts where we break things

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:05.790
down by response code,
by a number of unique IP

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.280
addresses, number of
visitors, by operating system,

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:10.790
by browser, and by their
resources, and the piece

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.000
that they're accessing.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.190
And actually at the
bottom here, we also

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.242
have the raw recruits
in this dashboard,

00:11:16.242 --> 00:11:17.700
we can both see
the visualizations,

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:18.950
[? where ?] we're aggregating.

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:22.830
But we can also see the raw
requests, the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.250
Now what I want
might want to do here

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.950
is because I saw quite
a number of 404 errors,

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:32.270
I might want to understand
better what led to these

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.630
and what these 404s even are.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.440
So one thing I can do
is I can filter down

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:38.015
to just the 404 errors.

00:11:38.015 --> 00:11:40.040
And I see an
interesting thing here,

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:42.480
where my number of
404 errors over time

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.530
were relatively constant
and relatively low,

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:46.310
and suddenly it spiked.

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.090
And that might be something
