00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:00.710
CHRISTOPHER WURM:
Yeah, thank you.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:01.500
Yeah, definitely.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.670
I mean, this is what
you need, right?

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:04.045
This is what you
need when you're

00:12:04.045 --> 00:12:07.331
doing some kind of root cause
analysis, some kind of data

00:12:07.331 --> 00:12:07.830
exploration.

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:09.590
You're trying to find out what's
happening with your system

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:10.090
right now.

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:11.420
And so speed is key.

00:12:15.612 --> 00:12:17.070
We can see all the
404 errors here.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.620
We have a spike
in the 404 errors.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.289
And that's something
interesting that I

00:12:20.289 --> 00:12:21.330
want to dive deeper into.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:23.621
And I could now do things
like look at the raw requests

00:12:23.621 --> 00:12:25.520
and maybe filter down even more.

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.430
But there's one more thing
that I want to show here

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:30.570
and that is how we
do anomaly detection.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.230
Anomaly detection, or we also
call it machine learning,

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:34.850
is one of the
commercial features

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.860
that we have for
enterprise users.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.190
And so I just want to quickly
show what that looks like.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.060
So for that, let's create one
of those anomaly detection

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.740
machining learning jobs.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.790
So what I can do here
in this interface is--

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:52.550
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So
before you create it,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.270
can you explain the
terminology here?

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:55.326
CHRISTOPHER WURM: Yeah.

00:12:55.326 --> 00:12:59.239
YURI GRINSHTEYN: What is a
job in the Elastic ecosystem?

00:12:59.239 --> 00:13:01.280
Because obviously, everyone
uses the term "jobs."

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.614
And everyone kind of attaches
a different meaning to it.

00:13:03.614 --> 00:13:05.196
CHRISTOPHER WURM:
That's a good point.

00:13:05.196 --> 00:13:06.056
That's a good point.

00:13:06.056 --> 00:13:07.712
Yeah, so a job in
that context is

00:13:07.712 --> 00:13:09.420
when we do anomaly
detection, you usually

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.360
want to do some kind of
analysis on your historical data

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:13.260
that you have.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.551
In this case, we already have
six weeks' worth of data.

00:13:15.551 --> 00:13:18.790
So we want to analyze these
six weeks' worth of data.

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:21.210
And now that is something
that we don't necessarily

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:22.582
want to do synchronously.

00:13:22.582 --> 00:13:24.040
But it's something
that [AUDIO OUT]

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:25.540
it happened in the
background asynchronously.

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:26.850
So that's what's called a "job."

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.641
Now everything else
that we've seen so for,

00:13:28.641 --> 00:13:30.930
every search that I've
done, the filtering, looking

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:32.940
at surrounding documents, the
dashboard, and the filtering

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.319
in the dashboard-- all of
that are synchronous requests.

00:13:35.319 --> 00:13:37.110
Like if I've done
something-- and then I've

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.260
had to wait a little
bit for it to show up.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.940
Now that works when I'm
doing the signal requests.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.730
But when I'm doing something
like anomaly detection on six

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:47.400
weeks' worth of data, then
no matter how fast we are,

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.780
it's going to take some time.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.446
And so that's why we
need to create job.

00:13:50.446 --> 00:13:52.257
And then the job runs
in the background

00:13:52.257 --> 00:13:53.340
to create these anomalies.

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:57.240
And that also just keeps running
as new data keeps coming in

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.490
and just keeps creating,
finding these anomalies.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.260
YURI GRINSHTEYN: OK, cool.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:00.780
Thank you.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:01.326
It helps.

00:14:01.326 --> 00:14:02.700
CHRISTOPHER WURM:
Yeah, no, sure.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:05.610
Thanks for asking.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:09.589
So here in this UI, we can
see how we create such a job.

00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:11.130
What we've tried to
do is we've tried

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:14.150
to abstract away a lot
of the complexities

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:15.120
of anomaly detection.

00:14:17.784 --> 00:14:19.950
Once, we tried internally,
[? as ?] somebody-- once,

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:21.360
the developers tried
internally to explain

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.318
to us how the anomaly
detection actually works,

00:14:23.318 --> 00:14:24.470
the math behind it.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:27.280
And well, I'm afraid I didn't
understand a lot of it.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:28.800
But what we tried
to do is we tried

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.920
to abstract away all of the
little complexities of it

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.285
and just give our
users a nice UI.

00:14:34.285 --> 00:14:39.090
And this was an API to use it
to make it usable for everybody.

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.250
Now in this case, I
have [? replica ?] data.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:43.680
So what I want to
look at is maybe

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:47.170
I want to look at the number
of visitors on my website.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:50.430
It's an important KPI for
every website to track that.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:53.580
And so I can see this
from my web logs.

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:57.960
What I have to do is let's
look at the unique count,

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.877
the distinct count
of IP addresses.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:08.020
And in a second here, we should
be seeing just the general

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:10.715
up and down that the
data like this shows us.

00:15:10.715 --> 00:15:12.715
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
again, while we're waiting,

00:15:12.715 --> 00:15:14.770
can you explain
what [INAUDIBLE]----

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.200
I see that you can click on
the little [? i's ?] to get

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:19.270
