00:17:01.612 --> 00:17:03.070
But let's look at
the results here.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:06.810
So if we look at
the results, let's

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:08.186
look at the very beginning.

00:17:08.186 --> 00:17:09.810
It's kind of interesting
to look at how

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:11.770
this anomaly detection works.

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:17.829
So if we can see here, the
first few days of data, we

00:17:17.829 --> 00:17:19.079
didn't really get a good read.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:20.828
The model, the shaded
area and background,

00:17:20.828 --> 00:17:22.170
is relatively imprecise.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.895
It just has an upper
and lower bound.

00:17:23.895 --> 00:17:26.400
But then as we kind of go
further into the future,

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:30.475
we can see how the model
tracks the data pretty well.

00:17:30.475 --> 00:17:32.100
YURI GRINSHTEYN: And
I mean, generally,

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.360
that's one of the
expectations you'll

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:34.110
have with machine learning.

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.930
[INAUDIBLE] the more data you
have, the more analysis you do,

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:38.846
the more precise your
results are going to be,

00:17:38.846 --> 00:17:40.650
the better the
trained the model is.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.855
And over time, your results
get better and better.

00:17:42.855 --> 00:17:43.980
So it makes a lot of sense.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:45.060
CHRISTOPHER WURM: Yeah.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:47.970
And so we can see here that it
has finally found the normally

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:49.630
that we were looking for.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:50.890
This is real data.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:54.090
So this actually happened
to us on-- this looks

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.020
